\f0\fs24 \cf0 Liquid Alchemy Recipe Content Standards\
\
Purpose\
\
Liquid Alchemy aims to preserve not only recipes, but also the context, provenance, and understanding that make those recipes meaningful.\
\
Not every cocktail will have information available for every category below. Missing information is acceptable. The goal is to capture as much verified and useful information as is reasonably available.\
\
A recipe should never be excluded because some fields are unknown.\
\
When referencing another named cocktail, place the relationship in the Riffs field whenever practical. Notes and Descriptions should primarily describe the current cocktail or craft item: its preparation, balance, ingredients, technique, service, usage, or educational value.\
\
Riffs are intentional relationship data. If a cocktail is related, inspired by, structurally similar to, derived from, commonly compared to, or descended from another cocktail, that relationship belongs in Riffs whenever practical.\
\
This preserves clean search behavior, creates explicit cocktail-to-cocktail relationships, and supports future discovery and navigation features.\
\
---\
\
Required Fields\
\
These fields should be present for every cocktail entry.\
\
Name\
\
Family\
\
Subfamily\
\
Recipe\
- Ingredients\
- Amounts\
- Units\
\
Technique\
\
Garnish\
\
Basic Lore\
- At minimum, a short description or explanation that helps the user understand the drink.\
\
---\
\
Strongly Recommended Fields\
\
These fields should be included whenever reasonably available.\
\
Creator\
- Individual creator, bar, organization, or publication associated with the drink.\
\
Source\
- Book, website, article, video, publication, or other source.\
\
Source URL\
- Link to the original source when available.\
\
Why It Matters\
- Brief explanation of why the cocktail is notable, influential, unusual, historically important, technically interesting, or simply worth knowing.\
\
Notes\
- Technique notes, ingredient guidance, substitutions, service notes, or other useful information about the current cocktail.\
\
---\
\
Provenance Fields\
\
Liquid Alchemy places special emphasis on provenance.\
\
Every cocktail should attempt to answer:\
\
"Where did this drink come from?"\
\
Provenance may take several forms:\
\
Historical Provenance\
- Creator\
- Bar\
- Publication\
- Date created\
\
Structural Provenance\
- Family lineage\
- Related cocktails\
- Architectural ancestors\
\
Ingredient Provenance\
- Ingredient innovations\
- Defining ingredients\
- Signature flavor relationships\
\
Personal Provenance\
- House creations\
- Event creations\
- Family recipes\
- Personal favorites\
- Creator cocktails\
\
Creator attribution should be included whenever it can be reasonably determined and verified.\
\
Creator attribution lends credibility to a recipe and pays homage to the people who created the cocktail.\
\
---\
\
Optional Enrichment Fields\
\
These fields add depth but are not required.\
\
Lore\
- Historical context\
- Stories\
- Interesting background\
\
Riffs\
- Variations\
- Descendants\
- Related cocktails\
- Structural relatives\
- Inspiration and lineage\
\
Alchemy Insight\
- Educational observations\
- Structural lessons\
- Technique insights\
- Ingredient relationships\
\
Craft Links\
- Homemade ingredients\
- Syrups\
- Infusions\
- Cordials\
- Preparations\
\
Tasting Notes\
- Flavor profile\
- Texture\
- Aroma\
- Finish\
\
Related Cocktails\
- Similar drinks\
- Ancestors\
- Descendants\
\
Creator Notes\
- Information about the creator\
- Additional creator context\
\
Citations\
- Sources supporting lore, history, provenance, creator information, or educational claims\
- Distinct from recipe source (where the spec itself originated)\
- Examples: Imbibe Magazine, Punch, Cocktail Kingdom, Death & Co., The PDT Cocktail Book, creator interviews, historical publications\
\
---\
\
Editorial Philosophy\
\
Not every cocktail has a rich documented history.\
\
Not every cocktail comes from a famous creator.\
\
Not every cocktail changed cocktail culture.\
\
Every cocktail comes from somewhere.\
\
Whenever possible, Liquid Alchemy seeks to preserve:\
\
- How to make the drink\
- Why the drink works\
- Where the drink came from\
- Who created it\
- What it connects to\
\
A cocktail is not valuable only because it appears in a famous book.\
\
Sometimes it is valuable because:\
- It was created for your daughter's event.\
- It became someone's everyday drink.\
- It reminds you of New Orleans.\
- It was made by a friend over a long weekend.\
- It's effing delicious.\
\
That's not database content.\
\
That's part of the culture of cocktails.\
\
The goal is not merely to collect recipes.\
\
The goal is to create a resource that rewards curiosity, preserves provenance, and deepens understanding.}